[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2608200100-a-healthy-port-is-not-your-server"
  :adr/title "A healthy port is not your server, and a store is named without its path"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-20"
  :adr/body
  "## The report

`cloud-itonami-app` を起動したあとの認証の手順が通らない。

## What was measured, 2026-08-20

Three data directories existed on this machine. One server.

| store | who resolves it |
|---|---|
| `~/.cloud-itonami/data` | the resident server, PID 3550, `CLOUD_ITONAMI_DATA_DIR` in its environment |
| `<checkout>/data` | `bin/itonami` run from the repository checkout |
| `~/.cloud-itonami/app/data` | a bare `clojure -M:cli` or `-M:server` in the resident app directory |

Each holds its own `agent-enrollment.key`. Presented to the running server
(label omitted, so nothing is minted and the key check is the only thing that
answers):

    ~/.cloud-itonami/data/agent-enrollment.key      -> label-missing   (the key was accepted)
    <checkout>/data/agent-enrollment.key            -> invalid-key
    ~/.cloud-itonami/app/data/agent-enrollment.key  -> invalid-key

`bin/itonami auth login` from the checkout therefore failed with
`enrollment key が一致しません`, and the same command from
`~/.cloud-itonami/app/bin/itonami` succeeded.

## Why it read as an authentication defect

Three things hid the real shape.

**Reads kept working.** The session token lives in ONE Keychain item
(`cloud-itonami-app.mcp` / `session-token`), not one per store. So
`itonami status` from the checkout listed the resident server's sessions while
printing `data-dir: <checkout>/data`. That output is a lie of exactly the kind
this repository refuses elsewhere: it names a store it is not reading.

**`clojure -M:server` in the checkout did not start anything.** It exits with
`BindException: Address already in use` — recorded four times in
`~/.cloud-itonami/data/server.log` and once in `target-server.log`. The
operator has started a server; the process answering 1338 is a different one.

**The probe could not tell them apart and answered as though it had.**
`server-process/ensure-running!` is documented as 'A server for this data
directory'. `healthy?` asked `/health`, and `/health` said `{ok, service,
schema}` — nothing about whose store. Something answering the port was taken
as the server for this store. This is root ADR-2608136000's shape: a check
that could not be made returning the value of a check that was made and passed.

The hosted half was not involved and was measured working:
`GET /api/auth/itonami/start` → 303 → `auth.itonami.cloud/authorize` → sign-in
page 200, and the token endpoint answers `invalid_grant` (not
`invalid_client`) for a bogus code, so the client and the localhost callback
are registered.

## Decision

1. **`/health` publishes `:store`** — `config/store-fingerprint`, twelve hex
   characters of SHA-256 over the canonical data-directory path. A fingerprint
   and not the path: the route takes no session, and the path names the
   operator's home directory to every caller that can reach the port. It is
   enough for the only question a client has, which is 'is this mine'.

2. **`server-process/store-agreement` has three outcomes, not two.** Nothing
   answering; answering but unable to say (a build older than the field);
   answering and either matching or not. 'Could not tell' is kept apart from
   'yes' — folding those together is what produced this — and also from 'no',
   which would strand every command against a server that is still running and
   still correct.

3. **`ensure-running!` refuses a known mismatch instead of adopting it.** It
   cannot start around one anyway: the port is taken. The refusal carries this
   process's data directory and store, the other store, and — when the resident
   layout accounts for it, confirmed by fingerprint equality rather than
   assumed — the directory to point `CLOUD_ITONAMI_DATA_DIR` at.

4. **`config/data-dir` resolves the resident install itself.** When the app
   directory is `~/.cloud-itonami/app`, the store is `~/.cloud-itonami/data`.
   `bin/itonami` already applied this rule by exporting the variable, which
   covered commands run through the launcher and nothing else — a bare
   `clojure -M:cli` in the same directory created the third store above. The
   rule belongs where 'which store is mine' is answered. It sits below both
   explicit answers, so an operator who names a directory still gets it.

5. **`itonami status` reports `:store`, `:server-store` and
   `:serves-this-store?`.** The two facts it used to print side by side as one.

## What this does not change

`CLOUD_ITONAMI_API_URL` still bypasses all of this. Pointing the CLI at a
control plane is an instruction, not an inference, and `remote?` already draws
that line for `stop!` — the local lifecycle is about the process that owns THIS
data directory, and a named remote has none.

Nothing about the authentication mechanisms. Passkey, hosted
`auth.itonami.cloud`, Email and SSO are untouched; so is the rule that an
agent session is rooted in reading a 0600 file (`agent-session`, ADR-0006).
The enrollment key was doing its job. The question 'whose store is this' was
never being asked.

## Verification

Each of the three changes was reverted alone and reddened only its own test:

    the refusal in ensure-running!    -> a-healthy-server-serving-another-store-is-refused
    :store on /health                 -> get-health-answers-the-liveness-contract
    the resident leg in data-dir      -> the-resident-app-directory-resolves-the-resident-store

The discriminating case in `store-agreement-test` is a server that is healthy
AND foreign: the test asserts `healthy?` returns true in the same breath as
`ensure-running!` refusing, so a revert cannot pass by claiming there was
nothing there. The 'cannot say' case asserts BOTH that it is not refused and
that it is not adopted as agreement — the two ways of collapsing three answers
into two. `config-test` covers the resident leg, the checkout that keeps its
own `./data`, an explicit answer overriding both, and that the fingerprint is
canonical, stable, and does not contain the path.

Whole suite: 1716 tests, 10241 assertions, 0 failures.

Measured live as well, against real processes rather than the stub:

  a second server on 1348 with its own store, asked from a third store
    -> the CLI printed both fingerprints, refused, and named what to set;
       `status` reported store f7a7622ada04 / server-store d1a153f3ce27 /
       serves-this-store? false, where before it printed one data-dir and the
       other store's sessions with nothing to tell them apart
  the resident server on 1338, which predates the field
    -> `/health` carries no :store, the command is NOT refused, and the
       enrollment failure comes back with this process's data directory and
       the reason the check could not be made

## Where this stands on this machine

Landed on `main` and pinned in west, and **not yet running**. The resident
server on 1338 was started before this and still answers `/health` without
`:store`, so on this machine every command still takes the 'cannot say' branch
— not refused, and explained only after an enrollment fails. It becomes the
'known' branch at the next restart of that process, which is the owner's call
while a native window and a workforce are attached to it.

The reading that follows from this: `curl localhost:1338/health` showing no
`:store` is evidence about the running build, not about whether the change
landed. Check `git log` or the west pin for that.

## Left alone

`~/.cloud-itonami/app/data` and `<checkout>/data` still exist on this machine.
They are the operator's, this ADR does not delete them, and after item 4 the
first can no longer be created. The virgin `<checkout>/data` still holds a
`user-1` with `:status :pending-passkey` and no membership — a server started
against it would ask for a Passkey and an organization from scratch, which is
the second face of the same confusion."}]
